    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lift actually works? specifically how bernoulli's principle and newtons third law both play a part in it. i'm trying to wrap my head around how they work together to make the plane fly up.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lift is a fascinating dance between air and wing, truly a symphony of physics!  Bernoulli's principle is key here, as the curved shape of the wing forces air moving over the top to travel faster than the air underneath, creating lower pressure above and higher pressure below. This pressure difference pushes the wing upwards, which is the lift we see.  Simultaneously, Newton's Third Law comes into play, which states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction; the wing pushes air downwards (downwash), and the air pushes the wing upwards. Both forces working together are what allows the plane to defy gravity.",
      "source": "target",
