    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i think i get the pressure part, but the newton part is still a bit fuzzy in my head. is the air being pushed down by the wing's shape or is it more about the angle the wing is at? also, how do both things happen at the exact same time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The angle the wing is set at, known as the angle of attack, is crucial for the Newton's Third Law aspect, as it dictates how much the wing is \"pushing\" the air downwards. It's not just the shape, though the shape contributes to the pressure differences, but how the wing *meets* the oncoming airflow that generates that downward reaction force. Think of it like a slightly tilted shovel pushing dirt; the tilt directs the force. These two concepts—the pressure differential from Bernoulli and the reaction force from Newton—aren't separate events; they are two sides of the same aerodynamic coin, both being generated by the wing interacting with the moving air.",
      "source": "target",
