    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes a lot of sense. using the shovel thing as an example helped. i guess i'm just curious if there's any way to visualize it better? like if you were trying to explain this to someone who isn't a math person, is there a way to describe that interaction between the air and the wing that isn't just using technical terms like pressure or downwash?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine the wing is like a very determined, slightly curved wall facing a stream of fast-moving water. The water rushing over the top has to speed up to clear the curve, making it less \"pushy\" than the water underneath. Meanwhile, the angle of the wall (the angle of attack) forces the water hitting the bottom edge to be deflected sharply downwards. It’s like the wing is constantly nudging the air out of its path, and the air, in turn, gives the wing a big, upward shove back! This combined push and pull is what makes the plane soar.",
      "source": "target",
